 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433671 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b65fa8f3-11c1-5f2d-b151-e84007d15bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-18{x}^{2}+26x-12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433673 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433673 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433673 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433673 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "879d12d0-2113-5e81-a5d2-2d55d4183634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433673 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $2,$ and the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }2}{\\text{factor of}\\,-4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $2$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433674 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433674 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433674 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433674 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433674 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433674 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433675 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433675 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433675 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18$ has a root in the interval $(2,4),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433675 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9c7c178-4ebe-5318-8cb7-742204e595a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433675 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x-3) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+3=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433676 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-8?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c76ce26c-3285-586a-b0c4-847ce7576f60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433676 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8,$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-4=-\\frac{4}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(4)$ is a factor of $-8$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433677 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433677 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433677 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433677 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+8$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7505886-69c0-558a-a441-b828788cc51e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433677 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 8,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
